  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will keep mostly quiet and dry conditions through\nearly Sunday. A dry cold front will pass through the region \nearly Thursday. The amount of mid-level dry air that was in\nplace will ensure a dry forecast despite a few spotty areas of\nslight chance showers. Low- level clouds will most likely make \nan appearance. The front should clear the Laurel Highlands \nbetween 8am and 11am Thursday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe current trends continue to support a tropical system coming\nashore and lifting north into the Ohio Valley on Sunday and\ncontinuing into Monday. This will bring rainfall north into the\nforecast area. Mean total rainfall amounts or roughly 48 hour\ntotals shows roughly 1.00\" to 1.25\" at this time point but\nwithin the quartile, being totally reasonable, this amount\nbecomes around 1.50\" This is still well below the FFG since we \nhave and will be dry for awhile before onset. The 90th\npercentile would hint at some minor issues in the northern WV\ncounties. A blocking trend of slow movement might change this \nbut for now, this event points to non-flooding rainfall for \nSunday and Monday. The severe potential is low at this time as\nthere is a 0% chance of 500 J/Kg or greater SB CAPE and even a\nprob of greater that 250 J/Kg is only 20% to 30% so will not\nhighlight a severe threat for now.  \n\nThe threat for moderate to heavy rainfall should diminish late\nMonday if the system tracks into our region, becomes absorbed\ninto stronger upper-level flow over Ontario, and a trough\nbuilds over the Great Lakes.",
